From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 87/90] Leave out the replies of commands the guest does not wait for

Guest-assigned creates and allocations, binds, submits with their staged command buffer
begins and ends, and host event operations are sent without waiting, but each reply still
crossed back to the process, counted against the deferred-reply bounds and was drained
before every synchronous command. With hello 0x800000 granted (with guest handles, handle
arrays, query pools, buffer views and the completion feed), each device stream switches to
silent success: at the first encoder lock hold once the feed runs, before anything that hold
sends, the encoder writes one REPLY_MODE packet, and from then on those commands queue no
reply (caboose-bounded-memory/silent_success.h). A failure arrives as a FAULT record on the
feed and is the loss of the process's device. Only encoders on a device stream switch; one
recording a command buffer into local staging never does. Under it the synchronous paths of
those commands are never taken: a command buffer begun or ended on its own does not wait, a
second encoder no longer turns their deferral off, every allocation is sent with its guest
handle (an import, export or allocation past the budget then waits for one round trip, and
one that would take its heap past its size counting this process's allocations is refused
locally with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY), and a pipeline create that may skip compilation
compiles instead. Before the presentation channel names objects, drainObjects waits for the
reply of one vkEnumerateInstanceVersion sent after the silent commands.
---
diff --git a/src/gfxstream/guest/vulkan_enc/ResourceTracker.cpp b/src/gfxstream/guest/vulkan_enc/ResourceTracker.cpp
--- a/src/gfxstream/guest/vulkan_enc/ResourceTracker.cpp
+++ b/src/gfxstream/guest/vulkan_enc/ResourceTracker.cpp
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include "ResourceTracker.h"
 #include "caboose-bounded-memory/deferred_destroys.h"
 #include "caboose-bounded-memory/guest_set_ids.h"
+#include "caboose-bounded-memory/silent_success.h"
 #include "caboose-bounded-memory/static_query_cache.h"
 #include "caboose-bounded-memory/source_identity.h"
 
@@ -4303,9 +4304,23 @@ static VkResult cabooseEncodeAllocateMemory(VkEncoder* enc, VkDevice device,
                 caboose::memory::asyncAllocationBudget.reserve(dev, heap, props.memoryHeaps[heap].size,
                                                                info->allocationSize);
     }
+    // Under silent success the encoder sends every allocation without waiting, and a refusal is
+    // device loss: a plain allocation that would take its heap past its size counting this
+    // process's own allocations alone is refused here, where the application sees it.
+    if (!async && plain && heap < props.memoryHeapCount && caboose::silent::replyMode().silent(enc) &&
+        info->allocationSize > props.memoryHeaps[heap].size -
+                                   std::min(props.memoryHeaps[heap].size,
+                                            caboose::memory::asyncAllocationBudget.committed(dev, heap)))
+        return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY;
     caboose::memory::asyncAllocation = async;
     VkResult result = enc->vkAllocateMemory(device, info, pAllocator, pMemory, true /* do lock */);
     caboose::memory::asyncAllocation = false;
+    // An allocation that would have waited for its reply (an import or export, or one past the
+    // budget) still waits under silent success until the phone has decoded it, so other channels
+    // can name it at once.
+    if (result == VK_SUCCESS && !async && caboose::silent::replyMode().silent(enc) &&
+        !enc->cabooseDrainDeferredObjects(true /* do lock */))
+        result = VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST;
     if (result != VK_SUCCESS) {
         if (async) caboose::memory::asyncAllocationBudget.cancel(dev, heap, info->allocationSize);
         return result;
